const agErosionPredict = {
  "q": "A field loses its topsoil to sheet erosion. What is the argument that finally moves a farm budget?",
  "choices": [
    "Erosion is only an aesthetic problem — the lower field receives the sediment",
    "Nutrients exported in eroded soil are fertiliser bought twice — applied, then exported",
    "Erosion costs nothing until the subsoil is exposed completely",
    "The sediment improves the nearest river's fertility, so the loss is recovered"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "Fertiliser bought twice: applied, then exported. Off-farm the sediment delivers that fertiliser to the nearest river, where it becomes the water lesson's eutrophication problem — soil conservation and water quality are the same budget."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/soil-health">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate agriculture lesson. It assumes the foundational lesson <em>Soil,
    Water, Sun - the Farming Equation</em> - crops treated as a resource system - and it is
    the natural companion to the intermediate <em>Plant Nutrition &amp; Fertilisers</em>
    lesson, which treats this lesson's soil as the receiving system. Everything else is
    built here from the ground up.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe how rock becomes soil, and why topsoil is effectively non-renewable on a human timescale;</li>
    <li>distinguish texture from structure and say which one management can change;</li>
    <li>read soil health in the field with a spade, an infiltration test and a slake test;</li>
    <li>explain field capacity, wilting point and plant-available water, and match irrigation methods to them;</li>
    <li>list the conservation practices that rebuild organic matter and slow erosion, and say why each works.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Soil health reframes dirt as <strong>a living system that degrades or regenerates depending
    on management</strong>. The farmer's real asset is not the land's area but its topsoil —
    centimetres of biology that took centuries to build and one bad storm to lose. The physics
    (structure, water) and biology (organic matter, microbes) are one system: manage either
    alone and the other quietly fails. This lesson covers the whole soil unit — how soil forms,
    what determines its type and texture, what makes it fertile, how water moves through it,
    and how erosion destroys what the rest took ages to assemble.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the biome, the components, the bank, the turn" widget="equationStep" config={soilCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four stages run from worldview to practice - soil as
    an ecosystem, soil as four components, organic matter as the bank account, and no-till as
    the management that stops overdrawing it. Notice that stage 2 draws the lesson's key line,
    texture fixed versus structure buildable, because almost every later recommendation
    follows from it.
  </p>

  <h2>How soil forms: rock into living skin</h2>
  <p>
    Soil is weathered rock colonised by life, assembled in layers over hundreds to thousands
    of years. Physical weathering (freeze–thaw, thermal cracking) shatters parent material;
    chemical weathering (rain's mild acidity dissolving minerals) releases the first nutrients;
    pioneer plants and lichens add organic matter, which holds water and accelerates the
    cycle. Given time, the profile differentiates into <strong>horizons</strong>: the dark,
    organic-rich <strong>A horizon</strong> (topsoil — the farming layer), the leached
    <strong>E horizon</strong> where present, the clay-and-mineral accumulating
    <strong>B horizon</strong> (subsoil), and the fractured parent <strong>C horizon</strong>
    below. The rate is the point: roughly a century per centimetre of topsoil under natural
    conditions — which is why erosion, running 10–100× faster on exposed farmland, is
    geological vandalism on a human timescale. Parent material, climate, organisms, relief and
    time (the five soil-forming factors) set each region's starting hand: volcanic ash makes
    fertile Andisols, tropical rainfall leaches ancient, acid, iron-rich Oxisols, grasslands
    build the deep black Chernozems of the world's breadbaskets.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="stack the profile back into place" widget="order" config={agHorizonOrder} />
  <Widget no="3" caption="guess the rate before the reveal" widget="estimate" config={agTopsoilEstimate} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "soil is a renewable resource, so losing some topsoil does
  not matter." Renewable only on geological time: topsoil forms at roughly a century per
  centimetre under natural conditions, while erosion on exposed farmland runs tens to hundreds
  of times faster. On a farm's planning horizon, topsoil behaves like a non-renewable asset -
  spent, it does not come back.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> soil is weathered rock colonised by life, assembled at roughly a
    century per centimetre - which is why an erosion rate tens of times faster is a debt no
    generation repays. What you farm, though, is not only the mineral hand you were dealt:
    the next section separates texture from structure.
  </p>

  <h2>Texture, structure and the four components</h2>
  <p>
    Mineral soil is graded into <strong>sand</strong> (2–0.05 mm — big pores, drains fast,
    holds little), <strong>silt</strong> (0.05–0.002 mm — smooth, holds moisture well) and
    <strong>clay</strong> (under 0.002 mm — flat plates with enormous surface area, holding
    water and nutrients chemically, but draining poorly and compacting). The proportions —
    <strong>texture</strong> — plot on the classic texture triangle (a loam is the balanced
    middle), and texture is essentially fixed: you farm the hand you are dealt.
    <strong>Structure</strong> — how those particles clump into <em>aggregates</em> — is not
    fixed, and it is where management lives. Good structure (crumbly, granular) holds big
    pores for air and drainage plus small pores for stored water; bad structure (compacted,
    slumped) does neither. The four components interlock: ideally about 45% mineral matter,
    5% organic matter, and the remaining half split between water and air whose proportions
    swing with every rain and every drying day. A soil's "tilth" — how it feels and works —
    is structure made tangible, and a spade is the first instrument: dark colour and crumb
    stability, roots running deep and sideways, worms in the spadeful, and no smeared shiny
    faces on the spade cut (a compaction signature) — field diagnostics before any
    laboratory.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "adding sand to clay soil 'improves drainage'." Adding a
    little sand to a lot of clay makes something worse than either — a dense, brick-like mix.
    Drainage is fixed with structure (organic matter, roots, avoiding compaction) or with
    engineered drainage, not by diluting the mineralogy.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — the clay-and-sand myth" widget="judge" config={agTextureJudge} />

  <h2>Soil temperature and the seasonal clock</h2>
  <p>
    The third pillar of the texture-and-conditions trio is <strong>temperature</strong>, and
    it is more manageable than texture. Soil temperature governs germination (each species
    has a minimum — wheat germinates near 4 °C, maize wants 10 °C and rising), microbial
    activity (nitrogen mineralisation roughly doubles per 10 °C within biological ranges —
    meaning fertiliser releases follow warmth), and root growth. Dark, moist, mulched soil
    warms slower by day and cools slower by night; a bare, dry seedbed swings violently.
    Plastic mulch is the deliberate exploit: clear plastic traps solar energy and pushes
    spring soil temperatures several degrees up, advancing sowings — the same physics in
    reverse (reflective mulch) cools summer root zones in hot climates. Depth matters: the
    top few centimetres ride the weather, while 30 cm down the temperature is nearly a
    monthly average — which is why gardeners learn to plant by soil thermometer, not by
    calendar, and why "sow when the soil, not the sky, is ready" is the oldest working
    advice in the craft.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="put maize sowing on the thermometer" widget="place" config={agGerminationPlace} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> texture and structure set the soil's physical housing, and
    temperature sets the biological clock that germination and microbes run on. The next
    section follows water through all of it - and water is where structure either performs
    or fails.
  </p>

  <h2>Water: infiltration, drainage, irrigation</h2>
  <p>
    Soil is the water cycle's filter and bank. When rain lands, everything hangs on
    <strong>infiltration</strong>: healthy aggregated soil drinks a downpour; compacted or
    crusted soil sheds it downhill — taking the topsoil with it:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="infiltration is a structure property — the same storm, two soils" widget="plotter" config={infiltCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> both soils face the same storm, but the green curve keeps
    drinking while the red one flattens early - the difference is structure, not rainfall. Once
    the red curve flattens, every further millimetre is running downhill looking for topsoil
    to take with it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once infiltrated, water is held against gravity at <strong>field capacity</strong> (small
    pores full, large pores drained) and extracted by roots until the
    <strong>wilting point</strong>, where the remaining film clings too tightly for plants to
    pull. The gap between the two is <strong>plant-available water</strong> — silt and
    well-structured loams hold the most; sand holds a little; pure clay holds much but grips
    it hard. Where rainfall cannot fill the gap, <strong>irrigation</strong> takes over, with
    an efficiency ladder: flood/furrow (traditional, 40–60% efficient), sprinklers (~70–85%),
    and drip lines (up to 95%, delivering water to the root zone drop by drop — Israel's
    breakthrough that made desert horticulture viable). Where water sits too long, the cure
    is <strong>drainage</strong> — ditches, tile drains, mole drains — because waterlogged
    soil kills roots by suffocation and blocks nitrogen cycling (waterlogged soils lose
    nitrogen to denitrification and turn grey-blue with reduced iron: a diagnostic you can
    see in any road cutting). Salinity is irrigation's shadow: every irrigation water carries
    dissolved salts that stay when the water evaporates — mismanaged schemes have salinised
    historic farmland from Mesopotamia to modern Australia, which is why irrigation always
    includes drainage planning.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="file each irrigation method by its efficiency band" widget="sort-buckets" config={agIrrigationBuckets} />

  <h2>Fertility: organic matter, pH and the nutrient bank</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Soil fertility</strong> is the capacity to supply what a crop needs, and it runs
    on three interlocking accounts. The <strong>organic matter</strong> account: decomposed
    plant and animal residue becomes <strong>humus</strong>, which holds up to ten times its
    weight in water, stores nutrients on its charged surfaces, feeds the microbial biome and
    glues aggregates together — it is the farm's slow-release asset, and conventional
    tillage has spent it down 30–50% in most old arable regions. The <strong>chemistry
    account</strong>: most nutrients reach roots only in solution, held exchangeably on clay
    and humus surfaces (cation exchange capacity — clay-rich, humus-rich soils hold the
    bank; sands leak it). And the <strong>pH gate</strong>: outside roughly 6.0–7.0, most
    crops cannot access the nutrients that are present — iron and phosphorus lock up in acid
    soils, molybdenum in alkaline ones — which is why liming acid soil (or sulfur-ing
    alkaline soil) precedes every fertiliser recommendation. Below pH 5.5, exchangeable
    aluminium turns soluble and poisons roots directly. Test before treating: pH, N-P-K and
    organic matter tests cost little and prevent the most common waste on small farms —
    blind fertilising of a field whose real constraint was acidity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="diagnose before you treat" widget="branch" config={agPhGateBranch} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if a crop looks hungry, the fix is fertiliser." Often the
  real constraint is the pH gate or the structure, not the supply: at the wrong pH, nutrients
  already in the soil are locked away, and blind fertilising of an acid field is the most
  common waste on small farms. Test before treating - pH, N-P-K, organic matter - and treat
  what the test shows.</p>

  <h2>The soil biome: the workforce</h2>
  <p>
    The biology is the difference between dirt and soil. <strong>Bacteria</strong> run the
    nitrogen chemistry (the fertiliser lesson picks this up); <strong>fungi</strong> build the
    architecture — mycorrhizal threads extend root reach ten-fold and pipe phosphorus to
    plants in trade for sugars; <strong>earthworms</strong> are the engineers, dragging
    litter underground, aerating with their burrows (Charles Darwin's last book was about
    them, and he measured their casting rates with patience worthy of the subject);
    <strong>protozoa and nematodes</strong> run the grazing food web that releases locked
    nutrients. Two partnerships matter commercially. Rhizobia bacteria in legume root
    nodules fix atmospheric nitrogen into plant-usable form — the biological engine of
    every rotation (a good legume stand can leave the equivalent of 100–200 kg of
    fertiliser nitrogen per hectare for the following crop). And mycorrhizae are damaged
    by deep tillage and long bare fallows, which is part of why no-till soils grow more
    resilient crops. A teaspoon of healthy
    soil out-organisms humanity by tens of billions — the workforce's size is why soil
    biology responds to management within a season, faster than chemistry or physics. The
    practical corollary: soil biology is fed through roots and residues, not through bags —
    which is why every soil-health prescription reduces, first, to "keep something growing
    here as much of the year as you can".
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="pair each member of the workforce with its trade" widget="match" config={agBiomeMatch} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "bags and bottles feed the soil biome." The workforce is
  fed through roots and residues, not purchased inputs - fertiliser changes the chemistry
  around it, but a soil starved of living roots and returned organic matter stays biologically
  poor at any dose. Keep something growing as much of the year as possible;
  that is the prescription nearly every other one reduces to.</p>

  <h2>Erosion and conservation: the final exam</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Water erosion</strong> (sheet, rill, gully) and <strong>wind erosion</strong> strip
    exactly the layer that everything above valued — and the historical record is blunt. The
    <strong>Dust Bowl</strong> (1930s US Southern Plains): prairie ploughed out, drought
    arrived, and "black blizzards" carried topsoil from Oklahoma to the Atlantic — 1935 alone
    removed an estimated 850 million tonnes; the Soil Conservation Service was created the
    same year. The fixes, then and now, form the conservation toolkit: <strong>keep the
    ground covered</strong> (cover crops between cash crops — living armour), <strong>keep
    roots alive year-round</strong> (feeding the biology, holding aggregates),
    <strong>disturb minimally</strong> (no-till drills seed through residue instead of
    ploughing), <strong>rotate and diversify</strong> (breaking pest cycles and spreading
    risk), and where slopes demand it, <strong>contour ploughing, terraces, strip cropping
    and windbreaks</strong> — each a way of shortening the run that water or wind gets. The
    conservation-agriculture triad (minimum disturbance, permanent cover, rotation) is the
    modern synthesis, and its measurable prizes are water infiltrating instead of running,
    organic matter rebuilding (soil carbon — the climate lesson's most discussed land
    lever), and diesel saved by ploughs that stay in the shed. The economics of erosion is
    the argument that finally moves budgets: nutrients exported in eroded soil are
    fertiliser bought twice — applied, then exported — and most of the world's cropland
    erodes faster than it forms. Off-farm, the sediment delivers its fertiliser to the
    nearest river, where it becomes the water lesson's eutrophication problem: soil
    conservation and water quality are the same budget, spent well or badly.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="bet before the economics lands" widget="predict" config={agErosionPredict} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Two neighbouring fields face the same storm; one sheds water and loses soil, the other drinks it. What property differs, and what management built it?</li>
    <li>Which holds more plant-available water, a sandy soil or a well-structured loam - and why is pure clay a poor answer?</li>
    <li>Why does adding a little sand to a clay soil make drainage worse, not better?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Structure: the healthy field has stable, crumbly aggregates
    with large pores for infiltration, built by organic matter, living roots and minimal
    tillage. (2) The loam: sand holds only a little water overall, while clay holds much of it
    but grips it too tightly for roots to extract - plant-available water is the gap between
    field capacity and wilting point, not the total held. (3) A little sand mixed into a lot
    of clay makes a dense, brick-like mix worse than either alone; drainage
    is fixed with structure - organic matter, roots, avoiding compaction - not by diluting
    the mineralogy.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Soil is a living system: centimetres of biology that took centuries to build and one bad storm to lose.</li>
    <li>Texture is fixed; structure is buildable - and structure decides infiltration, rooting and erosion resistance.</li>
    <li>Organic matter is the bank account: water held, nutrients stored, aggregates glued, biome fed.</li>
    <li>Plant-available water is the gap between field capacity and wilting point, not the total held.</li>
    <li>The pH gate sits under everything: a present nutrient is not necessarily an accessible one.</li>
    <li>Soil biology is fed through roots and residues, not bags - keep something growing.</li>
    <li>Erosion is fertiliser bought twice; conservation agriculture pays it back.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lessons <em>Plant Nutrition &amp; Fertilisers</em> and <em>Pests, Weeds &amp;
    IPM</em> both spend what this lesson banks - nutrients and a functioning root zone - and
    <em>Farm Economics</em> prices the erosion bill. The advanced <em>Agroecology</em>
    lesson widens the lens from the soil to the whole farm as one ecosystem.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative edges reward the effort. <strong>Compaction</strong> is silent theft: a
    single heavy machinery pass on wet soil can raise bulk density enough to halve
    infiltration and stall roots below the wheel track — controlled-traffic farming (all
    wheels on permanent lanes) and tillage radish (a cover crop that literally drills
    bio-pores) are the standard remedies. <strong>Slaking</strong> — aggregates collapsing
    when suddenly wetted — is the quick field test of structure that separates stable
    humus-glued soil from chemistry-glued soil. Organic matter rebuilds slowly and
    asymmetrically: a well-managed rotation might add a few tonnes of carbon per hectare per
    year early on, then plateau as the soil saturates — the honest limit of soil-carbon
    offsets. And the open questions are active: how deep does the "rhizosphere" economy
    run (half of soil's respiration is root-and-microbe trade), can regenerative systems
    match conventional yields while rebuilding the account (the evidence says: yes on
    diversified farms, with management skill as the deciding variable), and how much of
    agriculture's carbon bill can soils genuinely re-bank. The lesson beneath all of them:
    soil is the one production system where the factory can be rebuilt by its own
    products — every stalk returned is a deposit, and the account, unlike most, pays
    interest in water.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brady &amp; Weil, The Nature and Properties of Soils; USDA NRCS soil-health principles; Montgomery, Dirt (2007); Cambridge IGCSE Agriculture 0600, unit 2. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
